# Copy to .env.local for local development

# The MACHINE credential: CI, scripts, the scheduler, and the way into the
# console before the first operator account exists. People sign in with an
# email and a password instead — see `npm run operator -- add`.
AUDITOR_RUN_TOKEN=change-me-in-production-16
# CHAOS_ENABLED=true

# Signs operator session cookies. Keep it SEPARATE from AUDITOR_RUN_TOKEN:
# while they are the same value, rotating the machine token signs every human
# out, which is the exact coupling operator accounts exist to break.
# Unset falls back to AUDITOR_RUN_TOKEN and /api/ready warns about it.
# Rotating this is the deliberate "sign everyone out" lever.
# AUDITOR_SESSION_SECRET=

# Passkey sign-in. Both required, or passkeys are simply off and everyone
# signs in with a password — which is the right state for local development
# and for preview deploys, since a credential is bound to one origin and a
# production passkey could not work against a preview URL anyway.
#
# AUDITOR_RP_ID is the registrable domain; AUDITOR_RP_ORIGIN is the full
# origin the browser must report. The id has to be the origin's host or a
# parent of it, and a mismatch turns passkeys off rather than half-working.
#
# Neither may EVER be derived from a request header. `Host` and
# `x-forwarded-host` are attacker-controlled wherever the app can be reached
# without a trusted proxy, and a relying party taken from one of them lets an
# attacker have credentials minted against a domain they control. Same
# reasoning as AUDITOR_SELF_URL below, with a sharper consequence.
# AUDITOR_RP_ID=audit.example.com
# AUDITOR_RP_ORIGIN=https://audit.example.com

# Who activity is attributed to when the caller is a MACHINE rather than a
# person — CI, a script, the scheduler. A signed-in operator is recorded under
# their own name and account instead. Unset renders as "Operator".
# AUDITOR_OPERATOR_NAME=

# Pages audited per run before the journey is truncated. Default 20 — a
# starting point, not a measurement. `audit_page_cap_reached` says when it bit.
# AUDITOR_MAX_PAGES_PER_RUN=20

# How long the HTML_CodeSniffer second-opinion scan may run per page, in
# milliseconds. Default 15000. It shares the walk budget with the primary
# engine and can never gate a run, so on expiry the page keeps its axe
# results and simply has no second opinion (`htmlcs_scan_unavailable` in the
# logs) — a hung extra scanner must not starve the one that decides.
# AUDITOR_HTMLCS_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# Ceiling on audits started, per window. A run launches a browser and makes a
# model call, so without this a loop in a caller spends real money unattended.
# Global, not per operator: the bill is shared. Fails open if the counter is
# unreachable — a cost control that becomes an outage has made things worse.
# AUDITOR_MAX_RUNS_PER_HOUR=20
# AUDITOR_MAX_RUNS_PER_DAY=100

# Ceiling on journey PREVIEWS — the replay behind "Verify so far" — counted
# apart from audits so an operator iterating on a selector cannot spend the
# hour's audits. Browser time, no model call. Same windows, same fail-open.
# AUDITOR_MAX_PREVIEWS_PER_HOUR=40
# AUDITOR_MAX_PREVIEWS_PER_DAY=200

# Ceiling on DOCUMENT work — every inspection, conversion, repair and intake.
# Each launches a JVM (and a converter for Word) on a function that may run
# five minutes; without this a leaked token or a looping caller had nothing in
# the way. Its own counter, so "Inspect all unreviewed" over a 200-document
# inventory cannot spend a client's audits. Refused with 429 before anything
# is buffered or fetched. Same windows, same fail-open.
# AUDITOR_MAX_DOCUMENTS_PER_HOUR=500
# AUDITOR_MAX_DOCUMENTS_PER_DAY=2000

# Scheduled runs. Vercel Cron calls /api/cron/tick hourly and sends this as a
# bearer token; without it the tick refuses everything and no schedule fires.
# The tick dispatches each due journey to its own invocation rather than
# auditing them itself, so AUDITOR_SELF_URL is where it posts (inferred from
# VERCEL_URL when unset, and never from a request header — the machine token
# travels with it).
# CRON_SECRET=
# AUDITOR_SELF_URL=
# CRON_MAX_STARTS_PER_TICK=3

# How long a run may sit in `running` before it is treated as abandoned.
# Default 360 (maxDuration plus a minute of grace).
# AUDITOR_RUN_STALE_SECONDS=360

# Ephemeral in-process stores instead of Postgres. Test harnesses only —
# nothing persists. Never set this in a deployed environment.
# AUDITOR_STORE=memory

# The AI advisory pass runs through the Vercel AI Gateway. On Vercel the
# deployment authenticates with its own OIDC token and needs no key; locally,
# `vercel env pull` writes one valid for about a day. Unset, with no OIDC
# token either, means deterministic findings only — never a run failure.
# AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
# Which model, as a gateway `provider/model` string; `off` disables the pass.
# The free default is right for public sites and wrong behind a login: it
# advertises no data-handling guarantee, and the pass sends the accessibility
# tree of every page walked. Choose a model deliberately before auditing an
# authenticated journey, or set `off`.
# AUDITOR_ADVISORY_MODEL=minimax/minimax-m3-free

# Neon Postgres, injected by `vercel integration add neon` + `vercel env pull`.
# Required: there is no local fallback store. Apply the schema with
# `npm run migrate`.
# DATABASE_URL=

# Upstash Redis. The console sign-in throttle and the three budgets all count
# here; unset means they count in process memory, per instance, resetting on
# every cold start.
# KV_REST_API_URL=
# KV_REST_API_TOKEN=

# Vercel Blob, for durable run evidence. Unset keeps artifacts on local disk.
# Injected by `vercel blob create-store <name> --access private`. The store has
# to be private: this evidence is authenticated pages on a client's system.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# How long run evidence is kept, in days. Default 90 — chosen, not inherited:
# thirty expired the evidence behind a report while the client was still
# remediating from it; ninety covers a remediation cycle and a quarterly
# review. Not longer, because these are screenshots of authenticated pages
# holding real end-user data, and how long they exist is part of what a
# client agrees to. `npm run prune:artifacts` sweeps anything older.
# ARTIFACT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Largest document POST /api/documents/remediate will accept. Default 25MB.
# Checked against Content-Length before the body is buffered, and against the
# real size afterwards because Content-Length can lie. Bounds the COMPRESSED
# size — a .docx is a ZIP — so the conversion timeout is what bounds a bomb.
# AUDITOR_MAX_DOCUMENT_BYTES=26214400

# Credentials for authenticated journeys, one pair per client.
# A journey step names the reference ("acme"); the secret is resolved here,
# server-side, so it never travels in a request body or lands in a stored
# journey. Reference "acme" -> AUDIT_CREDENTIAL_ACME_USER / _PASS.
# AUDIT_CREDENTIAL_ACME_USER=
# AUDIT_CREDENTIAL_ACME_PASS=
